Background:

Most Bi platforms require dedicated executables: Tableau and PowerBI do. The object here is to do a BI Platform that's browser-based. 

Most BI platforms require connection to a database. One design goal is to read data from Parquet files on Amazon S3. Do whatever SQL needs to be done via DuckDB, running the browser under WASM. 

Ideally, this would all be done without a browser extension. 

The main external server would be a web server that can serve the javascript for the visualization needed. 

For larger customers, a secondary external server could deal with authentication: a connection to either Active Directory or LDAP to authenticate that this user has access to a given report or data set. 

The user interface would be some javascript graphing layer, there are a number of good ones. We'll try to use open source software as much as possible, so we could use D3.js, Apache Echarts, or some similar package. Normal charts: bar, pie, line, scatter, etc would be needed as well as sankey, raincloud and 3-D plots. Animated charts would be nice. 

There two parts to today's analysis: 
First: technical viability. Is a browser-based BI platform technically feasible? 
Second: if technically feasible, we need to produce a detailed specification so that it could be implemented either by human, AI coder, or some combination. 

Please ask any clarifying questions. 


